Package: ml216x-printer-app
Version: 2.0.0~alpha-6
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Architecture: amd64
Maintainer: Çağan ERGÜN <73942784+caganerg@users.noreply.github.com>
Depends: libc6 (>= 2.34),
         libgcc-s1 (>= 4.2),
         libpappl1t64 (>= 1.3.1), libpappl1t64 (<< 2.0),
         libcups2t64 (>= 2.4.10)
Recommends: cups, avahi-daemon
Provides: samsung-ml2160-rust
Conflicts: samsung-ml2160-rust
Replaces: samsung-ml2160-rust
Homepage: https://github.com/caganerg/ml216x-printer-app
Description: Printer application for Samsung ML-216x laser printers
 ml216x-printer-app is a standalone IPP Everywhere printer application for
 Samsung ML-2160, ML-2165, ML-2165W and ML-2168 monochrome laser printers. It
 converts the PWG Raster a client sends into the printer's native SPL2 /
 QPDL v3 format: PJL job envelope, 17-byte page header, Algo 0x11
 RLE-compressed band records and checksums.
 .
 It runs entirely in user space: a systemd user service in your own session,
 as your own user, with no root process and no setuid binary anywhere in the
 path. It serves IPP on the loopback address, advertises itself over DNS-SD,
 and prints to a printer reached by a socket:// or usb:// device URI that you
 choose yourself. Its state lives in your $XDG_CONFIG_HOME and its spool in
 your runtime directory. A packaged udev rule tags the hardware-confirmed
 Samsung 04e8:330f USB device for uaccess, so the logged-in user can open it
 without being in group lp. No CUPS filter and no PPD are installed: any IPP
 client, CUPS included, drives it over IPP.
 .
 This is a 2.0 alpha. The hard margins it uses are derived from the classic
 PPD and have not yet been measured on paper, which is release gate G-1 in the
 project's documentation. The 1.x CUPS raster filter it replaces is frozen at
 the v1.x-final tag, and its source is still in this package's source tree.
 .
 This package was called samsung-ml2160-rust up to 2.0.0~alpha-4, and was
 renamed with the repository. Both install /usr/bin/ml216x-printer-app and
 cannot be co-installed, so the Conflicts/Replaces/Provides triple above makes
 apt remove the old one; that removal is also what stops and disables its root
 system service, which this package must not race for port 8631.
 .
 Two dependency lines are stated by hand rather than derived. libcups2t64 is
 named because of risk R-6: PAPPL embeds the CUPS raster page header by value
 in the print-options structure it hands to this driver, so libcups defines
 part of the ABI compiled against, but the binary references no libcups symbol
 and no automatic tool would record it. libpappl1t64 is bounded to
 >= 1.3, < 2.0 (decision Q-1), which is tighter than the shared-library
 symbols alone would produce: dpkg-shlibdeps computes >= 1.0.1, while the
 behaviour this driver relies on — how a job's resolution is selected, and how
 system state is persisted — was read from the 1.3.1 source.
